spec:
  grid: 22 rows x 25 cols [observed]
  background: 1 (unique most-frequent color; also a valid color in the cycling tile)
  tile:
    structure: |
      Each column independently cycles through colors 1..K+1 with step +1 (mod K+1).
      tile[r][c] = ((r - offset[c]) mod (K+1)) + 1, where offset[c] is the row
      in which bg=1 appears in column c. Each column has exactly one bg cell.
    K: 5-8 (number of foreground colors, using colors 2..K+1)
    row_period: K+1 (6-9)
    col_period: 4-9, col_period <= row_period
    offsets: array of col_period values in 0..row_period-1; must have no sub-period
    fg_density_in_tile: K/(K+1) i.e. ~83-89%
  wallpaper: tile repeated over entire grid (partial tiles at edges)
  erasure:
    mechanism: some foreground cells replaced with bg=1
    blob_count: 3-6 connected components (4-connectivity)
    blob_size: 5-25 cells each (excluding singletons which are rare)
    total_erased: 8-15% of all foreground cells in the output
    no_fully_erased_residue_class: every (r%rp, c%cp) fg residue class retains >=1 visible instance
  solver_assertions:
    - bg is unique most-frequent color in input
    - smallest-area period candidate is unique (no ties)

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick K in 5..8; set rp=K+1; pick cp in 4..rp
    2. Generate offset[0..cp-1] in 0..rp-1 with no sub-period (reject if any divisor p of cp reproduces the full sequence)
    3. Build tile via tile[r][c] = ((r - offset[c]) % rp) + 1; wallpaper it over 22x25 grid = output
    4. Verify bg=1 is unique most-frequent color in output (guaranteed by construction)
    5. Choose 3-6 random rectangular regions (2-5 rows x 2-5 cols) scattered across the grid
    6. Within each rectangle, erase all fg cells to bg=1 (natural bg cells stay bg, creating sub-rectangular blobs)
    7. Verify total erased is 8-15% of output fg cells; verify no residue class fully erased; retry step 5-6 if violated
    8. The erased grid is the input
```
